          "text": "Of 30 ideas, two are worth a llame spike: the refusal registry (D1) and an MCP-driven acceptance run (C1). The rest either don't transfer or belong in solusnxt itself.\n\nEach idea was scored by whether a llame spike proves something true about solusnxt. That only holds where both repos run the same mechanism: Postgres semantics, OpenSpec, mutation testing, the agent workflow. The three ideas I developed further all lost transfer credit once they were checked against the code. The full pool with scores is in `scratchpad/solusnxt-spike-brainstorm.md`; the clusters are below.\n\n**Pool by cluster**\n- **A. Node transport and task lifecycle.** A1 pull-based lease queue (7.55), A3 parking tasks for offline nodes (7.25), A5 \"why is this stuck\" autopsy (6.95), A4 typed failure lane (6.65), A6 signed task envelopes (5.6), A7 discard ratio (5.1).\n- **B. Status truth (the \"status flash\" class).** B1 transition table plus reconciler (6.95), B2 versioned status projection (6.7), B3 status folded from receipt events (6.05), B4 full event sourcing (trap).\n- **C. Verification harnesses.** C1 acceptance run through MCP only (7.3), C2 deterministic simulation testing (6.95), C3 runbooks as tests (6.0), C4 degradation ladder (6.0).\n- **D. Authorization.** D1 refusal registry (8.05), D2 RLS in shadow mode (7.45, falls a lot after the deeper look), D3 authz decision log with policy replay (7.25), D4 batched resolver and D5 expiring grants (5.4 each).\n- **E. Release and upgrade.** E1 upgrade matrix (4 of 5 generators proposed it), E2 SLSA provenance, E3 seed bundles, E4 lineage stamps, E5 rolling DB dump. All score 4.4–5.4.\n\n**Shortlist**\n1. **D1, refusal registry.** A refusal is a behaviour the system must deny, such as a cross-tenant read. It ranks first because what it proves is process, and both repos already run OpenSpec and mutation testing.\n2. **C1, MCP-only acceptance (the non-obvious pick).** llame is already an MCP client and solusnxt already ships `laravel/mcp`, so llame can act as the test agent itself. It answers \"can an agent do every UI action through MCP?\" against a solusnxt dev instance.\n   - Hard limit: only a dev instance with synthetic data, and the model provider must be approved for corporate code and data. Without that, don't start.\n3. **B1+B2, status from a transition table plus a versioned projection.** This is a cheap refactor of llame's Run status, and the pattern carries to the status-flash class directly. Moderate confidence that llame shows the flash at all; check that first, or the spike proves nothing.\n4. **A3 or A1, cut down to the \"RabbitMQ as wake-up hint\" variant.** Postgres holds the durable task record and the broker only nudges nodes, so stranded tasks and broker/node reconciliation are fixed without removing RabbitMQ.\n\n**Traps**\n- **E1, upgrade matrix in llame.** llame has no releases, no customers and replaceable migrations, so it proves nothing about Laravel migrations or the installer. It is high value, but build it in solusnxt directly.\n- **D2 as a \"llame spike\".** The deeper look found it is really a scratch-database spike. The hard part is identity plumbing in Laravel, which llame doesn't have: no request-scoped transaction, plus queue workers, RabbitMQ consumers and Telescope. Transfer realism is low to moderate.\n- **F3 Kanban lanes** (solo repo, no team to measure), **B4 event sourcing** (rewrite cost), **E2 SLSA on GitHub Actions** (solusnxt CI is Jenkins), **A7 discard ratio**.\n\n**Deeper looks**\n\n**D1, refusal registry.** Moderate transfer realism.\n- How it works:\n  - Add `Refusal: <clause-id>` markers to the existing OpenSpec scenarios, for example `tenant.cross-read` or `perm.bypass-denied`.\n  - Tag negative tests with the clause id.\n  - `scripts/check-refusal-contract.mjs` fails on a clause with no test, a dangling test tag, or a tag for an unregistered clause.\n  - Each clause lists its guard code (the function that does the refusing), so mutation testing targets those ranges instead of the whole diff.\n- Already in llame: the scenarios in `openspec/specs/*`, more than 80 integration tests, and `scripts/mutation-changed-lines.mjs`. Missing are the ids and the links between them.\n- Transfers: the marker format, the linker and the guard-targeted Infection filter. Clause content does not; solusnxt needs node scope, signed update and backup, and admin-vs-user scoping.\n- Main risk: a tag only proves a test exists. Mutation testing handles deny branches poorly, because of equivalent mutants and shared middleware.\n- Better variation: a sabotage gate. CI flips the guard to allow-all through a test-only seam and requires the linked tests to go red. That is cheaper, deterministic, and works for the Go agent too.\n- First step: one capability (`tool-call-permissions`), 3–5 tagged tests, the check script wired into `pnpm lint`. Then run Stryker once by hand on the guard files and count survivors; that number decides whether the mutation half is worth automating.\n- Payoff: a clause-by-test matrix that counts as evidence in solusnxt security review, cutting the per-change review cost.\n\n**A1+A3, pull-lease node queue.** About 40% of the value transfers.\n- What transfers: the SKIP LOCKED, lease and idempotent-completion semantics (identical SQL on PG18), the state machine, and the chaos test matrix.\n- What doesn't: the Go agent, mixed-version upgrades across customers, scale (llame has a handful of nodes; a deployment has tens to hundreds, with PHP polling load and a hot task table), and the org decision. RabbitMQ was just upgraded under SVM2-6737.\n- It is also ahead of llame's own roadmap: Node (#755) is in the backlog, and remote dispatch waits on #756.\n- First step: no llame infrastructure. Write a throwaway SQL prototype as an integration test with a SIGK********ease case. Take the acceptance cases from the stranded-task incidents in your notes.\n- Most adoptable variant: wake-up-hint-only, or just a deliver-by deadline plus a per-type policy on the backend. That fixes the 24h \"Updating\" problem with no agent change.\n\n**D2, RLS in shadow mode.** Low to moderate transfer realism.\n- Postgres has no audit-only RLS. The workable shadow is a second-pass differ:\n  1. Capture the SQL with `DB::listen`.\n  2. Re-run it on a NOBYPASSRLS role with transaction-local `set_config`.\n  3. Diff primary-key sets in both directions, sampled, off the hot path.\n- Two hazards:\n  - Identity plumbing: Laravel doesn't run each request in a transaction, and workers have no user, so they need an audited system identity or it becomes the bypass.\n  - The admin/reseller hierarchy needs a function returning the ids a user may see. Per-row recursive CTEs inside policies destroy the query plan.\n- llame contributes only the migration template and the test pattern.\n- The cheaper win: the negative-isolation test generator. That is the BOLA sweep from the last round, compared on a bypass connection and an RLS connection.\n\n**Wildcard for a next pass:** which solusnxt pains does llame reproduce with the same mechanism, not just the same idea? That list is about five items: Postgres semantics, OpenSpec, mutation gates, the agent dev loop, MCP. Every idea outside it is advice wearing a spike's clothes. Turn it around: what can llame consume from solusnxt? `laravel/mcp` is the only live bridge, and nothing has used it yet.\n\n**By leverage**\n1. D1 first step: one capability, the check script, one manual Stryker count. Hours, and it transfers as process.\n2. Settle the provider and data-policy question for C1 before writing any code. If it's blocked, drop C1.\n3. Check whether llame's Run status actually flashes. Spike B1+B2 only if it does.\n4. Write the A1/A3 failure-mode matrix from your incident notes, and take the \"deliver-by plus per-type policy\" variant to solusnxt directly. It needs no llame spike.\n5. Build E1, the upgrade matrix, in solusnxt rather than llame. Highest value in the pool, wrong place to spike it.\n\nSay which to start and I'll open the llame issue for it.",
