            "content": "# solusnxt: gates that make AI-written code trustworthy\n\nRecon date 2026-10-18, read-only, against the local checkout.\n\n## Thesis\n\nStricter lint on its own does not make AI-written code trustworthy. An agent\nsatisfies any lint rule by adding an ignore comment, widening a type to `mixed`\nor `any`, regenerating the baseline, or writing a test that asserts nothing. So\nthe gates that matter are the ones an agent cannot pass by editing the gate:\n\n- ratchets on suppressions and baselines;\n- mutation scores with honest accounting;\n- contract conformance checked inside the tests;\n- sabotage tests;\n- replaying past defects.\n\nLint is the floor, not the mechanism.\n\n## What solusnxt already has (do not re-propose)\n\n- PHPStan at level 5 with larastan, a 1,323-entry baseline (984 of those entries\n  are `declareStrictTypes`), and a strong custom suite in\n  `backend/codingStandard/PHPStan`: protected models and tables, forbidden base\n  classes, relation generics, and `reportWrongPhpDocTypeInVarTag` plus\n  `reportAnyTypeWideningInVarTag`.\n- Infection filtered to diff lines, run by `development/ci/cmd/mutationTesting.go`,\n  which posts surviving mutants to the PR.\n- Rector dry-run, php-cs-fixer and PHPStan in `.github/workflows/_lint-php.yaml`.\n- Claude hooks: `post-edit-fmt.sh`, and `stop-lint-check.sh`, which dispatches\n  checks by component against the merge base.\n- `phpunit.xml`: `failOnRisky`, `failOnWarning`, random execution order.\n- Frontend: `strict` tsconfig and `no-explicit-any` set to error.\n- Go: golangci with about 170 config lines per module.\n- Schemathesis, upgrade-stack tests and installer tests in Jenkins.\n- Leo's own `php-tooling-analysis.md` already plans Goal 2 (cognitive-complexity\n  and disallowed-calls), Goal 3 (mutation gate), Goal 6 (deptrac) and Goal 8\n  (OpenAPI in feature tests). The status checks below show Goals 2, 6 and 8 are\n  still not adopted in `composer.json` or the tests.\n\n## Measured holes\n\n| signal | count |\n|---|---|\n| `@infection-ignore` (backend) | 47 |\n| `@phpstan-ignore` (backend) | 13 |\n| `markTestSkipped/Incomplete` | 8 |\n| `eslint-disable` (frontend) | 384 |\n| `as any` / `: any` (frontend) | 125, despite `no-explicit-any: error` |\n| `@ts-ignore/@ts-expect-error` | 29 |\n| `.skip` / `xit` (frontend) | 11 |\n| `//nolint` (Go, excluding vendor) | 602 |\n\nNothing in CI, `development/ci/cmd` or the hooks ratchets any of these. A\n`baseline` match appears only in deploy code.\n\nThe diff-line mutation score counts **timed-out and errored mutants as\ndefeated** (`mutationTesting.go:497-499`). An earlier note measured about 83% of\nmutants timing out, so the changed-lines score the agent sees is inflated by\nconstruction. This is the most direct reason the current gate cannot be trusted.\n\n## Gates, ranked by leverage\n\n### G1 — Ratchet on suppressions and baselines (this week)\n- Mechanism:\n  - A CI step and the Stop hook count the markers above per component, plus the\n    PHPStan baseline entry count, and compare them with the merge base.\n  - Any increase fails.\n  - A new suppression must carry a reason and a ticket id. Use PHPStan's\n    `@phpstan-ignore identifier (reason)` form, eslint's\n    `-- reason` description, and `//nolint:linter // reason`.\n- Why it ranks first: it closes the cheapest escape hatch an agent has, and\n  every later gate depends on it.\n- Cost: about 50 lines of Go in the existing `development/ci` tool, plus one\n  line in `stop-lint-check.sh`. Existing debt stays where it is, and nothing\n  breaks.\n- Ready artifact: llame's `no-untracked-todo` and its \"lint rejects unused\n  disables\" rule (`--report-unused-disable-directives` in eslint,\n  `reportUnmatchedIgnoredErrors` in PHPStan, on by default).\n\n### G2 — Honest mutation accounting (this week)\n- Mechanism:\n  - Stop counting `TimeOuted` and `Error` as defeated in the diff-line score.\n  - Report the timeout share separately, and fail when it is above a threshold.\n  - Lower Infection's per-mutant timeout, or set `timeoutsAsEscaped`, so\n    timeouts stop hiding escapes.\n  - Confirm whether the gate blocks a PR or only comments. Reading the code, it\n    computes the score and posts comments; I did not find a hard fail, so\n    verify before relying on it.\n- Why: the mutation score is the only signal that tests detect anything. As it\n  stands, a test suite that hangs reads as a strong one.\n- Cost: a few lines in `filterReport`, plus one calibration run to re-baseline\n  the threshold.\n\n### G3 — OpenAPI response validation inside feature tests (Goal 8)\n- Mechanism: a base `TestCase` hook validates every JSON response against the\n  spec with `league/openapi-psr7-validator`. Opt out per test only with a reason.\n- Why: AI code drifts from the spec, and Schemathesis finds drift late and\n  without saying which change caused it. This puts the failure on the test that\n  exercised the change.\n- Cost: medium. The first run will surface the existing drift, so start in\n  report-only mode and switch to a ratchet once that list is triaged.\n\n### G4 — Coverage test for route authorization\n- Mechanism:\n  - A test reads `route:list --json`.\n  - Every `api/*` route must have auth middleware, plus either a `FormRequest`\n    whose `authorize()` is not `return true`, a policy or `authorize()` call\n    reachable from the controller method, or an entry in a reviewed allowlist\n    with a reason.\n  - The allowlist only shrinks (ties into G1).\n- Why: forgetting authorization is the AI defect with the highest impact under\n  solusnxt's USER_EXPLOITABLE category, and nothing currently checks for it\n  structurally.\n- Extension: the refusal registry and sabotage test from the earlier brainstorm.\n  Flip the policy to allow-all through a test seam, and require the linked\n  negative tests to fail.\n\n### G5 — Land Goal 2 plus `phpstan-strict-rules`, as a ratchet\n- Mechanism:\n  - Add `tomasvotruba/cognitive-complexity` with function and class budgets.\n  - Add `spaze/phpstan-disallowed-calls`: `env()` outside config, `dd`, `dump`,\n    `DB::raw` and `whereRaw` with interpolated strings, `Http::` without\n    `timeout()`, `sleep`, `app()`/`resolve()` service location, and `Log::`\n    calls with secret-shaped keys.\n  - Add `phpstan/phpstan-strict-rules`.\n  - Baseline the existing violations; G1 keeps the baseline from growing.\n- Level 5 → 6 and beyond: use the per-identifier baseline-diff method from\n  `svm2-6707-phpstan-baseline-plan.md`. Variant: a second config,\n  `phpstan-new.neon`, runs at level max on **files added in the PR** only, so\n  new code starts strict and the legacy debt does not block it.\n\n### G6 — Static analysis of tests, aimed at tautological AI tests\n- Mechanism:\n  - `phpstan.neon` analyses only `app/` and `api/`; add `tests/` with a small\n    rule set.\n  - Custom rules: no `assertTrue(true)`; no test whose only assertion is\n    `expectException` with a generic `\\Exception`; no mocking the class under\n    test; no assertion on a value the test itself just set.\n  - These port llame's `no-vacuous-throw-assertion` and `no-module-mocking`.\n  - Add `beStrictAboutCoverageMetadata` and `beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests`.\n- Why: a test can pass the mutation gate on one file and still prove nothing\n  about behaviour. These rules catch the common shapes cheaply.\n\n### G7 — Gate-recall benchmark (the \"evals\" ask)\n- Mechanism:\n  1. Mine `fix:` commits and incident notes (SEC-76821, svm2-7038,\n     security-review-findings, the 6737 regressions).\n  2. For each, check out the parent commit with the bug present and run the\n     current gates.\n  3. Record which gate caught it, or none.\n  4. Run this nightly in Jenkins.\n- Output: a recall table per gate. It decides which of G3–G6 to fund next with\n  data, and it measures trust directly instead of asserting it.\n- Second half: turn the same corpus into a Claude Code agent eval\n  (`claud